dash='## Quant & Market Dashboard\n\n### Liquidity and volatility\n\n- **Overnight:** Futures show divergence at the story’s 05:04 CST checkpoint. Current ES/NQ spreads, depth, overnight volume, ETF executable quotes and Treasury-market depth are unverified; price direction is only a cautious sentiment proxy. No reliable current liquidity rating can be assigned.\n- **Funding:** Latest verified SOFR is 3.89% for November 6, updated November 9 at 07:02 CST. Today’s update was not available at cutoff. This lagged funding reading is not a current stress diagnosis. [FRED SOFR](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/SOFR).\n- **Regime: Moderate; medium confidence, with concentrated CPI event risk.** Delayed VIX was 20.04 at November 10 06:32 EST / 05:32 CST, versus November 9 close 19.92 and November 6 close 25.64. House thresholds: below 15 low, 15–25 moderate, 25–35 high, above 35 stressed; not calibrated forecasts. Current skew, term structure and dealer gamma remain unverified. [VIX](https://chartexchange.com/symbol/index-vix/historical/).\n\n### Factor proxies\n\n| Factor | ETF | Nov 6→9 | Oct 9→Nov 9 (~1 month) |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Momentum | [MTUM](https://stockanalysis.com/etf/mtum/history/) | +4.88% | -16.41% |\n| Value | [VLUE](https://stockanalysis.com/etf/vlue/history/) | +2.72% | -6.20% |\n| Quality | [QUAL](https://stockanalysis.com/etf/qual/history/) | +2.21% | -1.30% |\n| Size | [IWM](https://stockanalysis.com/etf/iwm/history/) | +3.23% | +1.28% |\n| Growth | [IWF](https://stockanalysis.com/etf/iwf/history/) | +2.92% | -10.09% |\n| Low volatility | [SPLV](https://stockanalysis.com/etf/splv/history/) | +1.25% | +7.89% |\n\nPrice returns calculated from source-linked split-adjusted Close columns, excluding distributions, through the November 9 cash close (16:00 EST / 15:00 CST). ETF proxies are not isolated factor premia. Common endpoints were rechecked today; independent corporate-action validation remains incomplete. All six rose Monday; momentum and growth remain negative over the longer window while low volatility and size are positive.\n\n### Execution and microstructure\n\n- Today, Tuesday November 10: CPI at 07:30 CST / 13:30 UTC; cash opening auction at 08:30 CST / 14:30 UTC. Keep pre-release and post-release observations separate. [BLS calendar](https://www.bls.gov/schedule/2026/11_sched_list.htm).\n- For SPY/QQQ, inspect quote age, NBBO spreads, displayed depth, auction imbalances and ES/NQ basis. Use bounded limits and participation caps; stress slippage, gaps, cancellations, missed fills and observation latency. Thin-depth rebounds can trap marketable orders.\n- Options: verify expiry, deliverables and settlement, including same-day expiries; reject stale, crossed or one-sided quotes. Gamma can amplify near-expiry hedging and implied volatility can reprice after CPI, but dealer-gamma sign, expiry concentrations, pinning and their magnitude are unverified.\n\n**Research watchlist:** SPY, QQQ, IWM, RSP, TLT; ES, NQ, ZN, VX; MTUM, VLUE, QUAL, IWF, SPLV; CRWV, NVDA.\n\n### DRL Improvements for the MATLAB System\n\n1. Enforce drawdown, exposure, participation and stale-data limits outside the policy, with an explicit safe fallback.\n2. Penalize spread, impact, turnover, latency and missed fills; evaluate tail losses separately from average reward.\n3. Use purged walk-forward evaluation across inflation-event and quiet regimes, with a one-use final holdout and all preprocessing fitted on training folds.\n4. Randomize spread, depth and observation delay; test risk compliance and abstention under regime shifts and liquidity withdrawal.\n\n### Model hygiene\n\nAudit leakage, survivorship bias, look-ahead, corporate actions, stale options quotes, multiple testing, calibration and drift. Use point-in-time universes, release vintages and actual availability timestamps. Isolate model selection from final evaluation and compare rank and threshold implementations on a common adjustment basis.\n\n### MATLAB optimization patterns\n\n- Use named-zone timetables and causal synchronize/retime; never backfill unreleased CPI observations.\n- Vectorize rolling ranks, preallocate logs and profile before optimizing.\n- Parallelize independent folds or Bayesian trials with reproducible random streams and fixed budgets.\n- Use GPUs for sufficiently large training batches; keep data, risk controls and evaluation in modular environments.\n\n### High-value quant research question\n\n**Do smooth rank-product factor weights reduce net trading costs versus Boolean screening intersections at matched risk, especially around CPI releases?**\n\nCompare identical point-in-time universes and risk budgets with purged walk-forward testing, realistic impact and missed fills. This tests whether threshold-driven turnover consumes apparent interaction alpha rather than assuming a smoother rule is better.\n'
